1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,762
In March 2023, a
photo of the Pope in a

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,142
white puffer jacket
fooled millions of

3
00:00:06,188 --> 00:00:06,699
people.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,209
It was not real.

5
00:00:08,627 --> 00:00:09,707
Nobody had taken it.

6
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:11,994
A piece of software
had imagined it.

7
00:00:12,575 --> 00:00:14,560
Three years later,
that is the least

8
00:00:14,606 --> 00:00:16,534
impressive thing
artificial intelligence

9
00:00:16,557 --> 00:00:17,207
can do.

10
00:00:17,857 --> 00:00:20,552
So what changed, how
fast, and what is

11
00:00:20,587 --> 00:00:21,446
still coming?

12
00:00:22,015 --> 00:00:24,255
To make sense of it,
we need one simple

13
00:00:24,302 --> 00:00:26,392
idea: the S-curve.

14
00:00:26,972 --> 00:00:29,457
Almost every
technology grows in three

15
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:30,234
phases.

16
00:00:30,652 --> 00:00:32,185
First comes a
slow start:

17
00:00:32,661 --> 00:00:35,831
years of research,
clumsy prototypes and

18
00:00:35,994 --> 00:00:37,317
very few users.

19
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:39,930
Then comes the steep
part, where

20
00:00:39,953 --> 00:00:42,321
everything improves
at once and the world

21
00:00:42,379 --> 00:00:44,121
changes in just
a few years.

22
00:00:44,661 --> 00:00:45,346
And finally

23
00:00:45,764 --> 00:00:47,099
the curve flattens.

24
00:00:47,518 --> 00:00:50,014
The technology
matures, and each new

25
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:51,709
version is only a
little better than the

26
00:00:51,755 --> 00:00:52,289
last.

27
00:00:52,853 --> 00:00:54,757
Mobile phones are
a clean example.

28
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:57,335
For years they had
tiny screens, no apps

29
00:00:57,358 --> 00:00:59,076
and a battery that
died by lunch.

30
00:00:59,659 --> 00:01:01,843
Then the smartphone
arrived, and within a

31
00:01:01,936 --> 00:01:04,455
decade it was in
nearly every pocket.

32
00:01:04,618 --> 00:01:06,940
Today, a new model
looks almost the same

33
00:01:06,963 --> 00:01:07,834
as last year's.

34
00:01:08,363 --> 00:01:09,674
Flight is another
one.

35
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:12,396
In 1903, the Wright
brothers stayed in

36
00:01:12,431 --> 00:01:14,079
the air for twelve
seconds.

37
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:16,692
Sixty-six years
later, people walked on

38
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:17,343
the Moon.

39
00:01:17,891 --> 00:01:19,934
Artificial
intelligence spent decades on

40
00:01:19,969 --> 00:01:21,363
the flat part of
that curve.

41
00:01:21,917 --> 00:01:23,043
Chess programs.

42
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,296
Spam filters.

43
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:26,386
Navigation apps that
find the fastest

44
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:26,816
route.

45
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:29,058
Recommendations that
know what you want

46
00:01:29,104 --> 00:01:30,010
to watch next.

47
00:01:30,572 --> 00:01:33,788
In 1997, IBM's Deep
Blue beat the world

48
00:01:33,835 --> 00:01:34,636
chess champion.

49
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:37,887
In 2016, AlphaGo beat
one of the best Go

50
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:38,816
players alive.

51
00:01:39,013 --> 00:01:40,964
Impressive, but each
system could do

52
00:01:41,057 --> 00:01:42,520
exactly one thing.

53
00:01:43,182 --> 00:01:46,886
Then, in November
2022, ChatGPT arrived.

54
00:01:47,048 --> 00:01:48,523
Within about two
months it reportedly

55
00:01:48,581 --> 00:01:50,787
passed one hundred
million users, one of

56
00:01:50,822 --> 00:01:52,934
the fastest growing
apps ever recorded.

57
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,126
That was the moment
the curve bent

58
00:01:55,184 --> 00:01:55,637
upward.

59
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,332
And it has not
stopped bending.

60
00:01:57,931 --> 00:01:59,266
Start with images.

61
00:01:59,499 --> 00:02:02,436
In 2022, AI pictures
were a curiosity:

62
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:05,095
melted faces, hands
with seven fingers,

63
00:02:05,164 --> 00:02:06,836
and text that looked
like a dream of

64
00:02:06,894 --> 00:02:07,614
text.

65
00:02:08,374 --> 00:02:10,407
Within two years,
the best image

66
00:02:10,442 --> 00:02:12,148
generators were
producing pictures most

67
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:14,029
people could not tell
from photographs,

68
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:16,746
with correct
reflections, believable skin

69
00:02:16,978 --> 00:02:18,731
and readable signs.

70
00:02:19,243 --> 00:02:21,484
That is a gift for
designers and small

71
00:02:21,542 --> 00:02:22,332
businesses.

72
00:02:22,692 --> 00:02:24,329
It is also a problem.

73
00:02:24,991 --> 00:02:26,965
When a convincing
image costs nothing and

74
00:02:27,023 --> 00:02:29,264
takes seconds, seeing
is no longer

75
00:02:29,322 --> 00:02:29,937
believing.

76
00:02:30,599 --> 00:02:32,363
Standards like
content credentials try to

77
00:02:32,421 --> 00:02:34,720
label how a file was
made, but adoption

78
00:02:34,778 --> 00:02:36,660
is still patchy, and
the fakes are

79
00:02:36,695 --> 00:02:38,378
improving faster than
the labels.

80
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:41,014
Video was supposed to
be the hard one.

81
00:02:41,676 --> 00:02:44,056
In 2023 the internet
laughed at a clip of

82
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:45,704
a famous actor eating
spaghetti that

83
00:02:45,762 --> 00:02:46,877
looked like a
nightmare.

84
00:02:47,237 --> 00:02:48,201
Faces melted.

85
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:50,059
Noodles had a mind
of their own.

86
00:02:50,639 --> 00:02:52,346
Two years later,
new models were

87
00:02:52,381 --> 00:02:54,296
generating clips with
realistic physics,

88
00:02:54,494 --> 00:02:56,270
smooth camera moves
and, for the first

89
00:02:56,305 --> 00:02:58,348
time, synchronized
voices and sound

90
00:02:58,395 --> 00:03:00,972
effects, all from a
single sentence.

91
00:03:01,535 --> 00:03:03,255
Today a short film
can be drafted in an

92
00:03:03,301 --> 00:03:04,137
afternoon.

93
00:03:04,282 --> 00:03:06,627
One person with a
laptop can prototype

94
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:08,566
scenes that once
needed a crew, a

95
00:03:08,647 --> 00:03:10,122
location and
a budget.

96
00:03:10,702 --> 00:03:13,117
The tools also
changed existing footage:

97
00:03:13,384 --> 00:03:15,660
swapping a character
into a scene, fixing

98
00:03:15,706 --> 00:03:17,576
where an actor looks,
turning a few

99
00:03:17,623 --> 00:03:19,643
photos into a 3D
space you can walk

100
00:03:19,689 --> 00:03:20,130
through.

101
00:03:20,662 --> 00:03:22,171
The flip side
is obvious.

102
00:03:22,403 --> 00:03:24,238
Anyone can now
fabricate a video of

103
00:03:24,296 --> 00:03:26,258
anyone saying
anything, which is why

104
00:03:26,328 --> 00:03:28,464
platforms ask
creators to label AI-made

105
00:03:28,487 --> 00:03:30,613
scenes, including a
few in this video.

106
00:03:31,203 --> 00:03:32,318
Voices are next.

107
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:34,303
A few seconds of
recording is now enough

108
00:03:34,338 --> 00:03:36,126
to clone someone's
voice well enough to

109
00:03:36,196 --> 00:03:37,066
fool a friend.

110
00:03:37,608 --> 00:03:39,187
That makes dubbing a
film into thirty

111
00:03:39,257 --> 00:03:41,405
languages, in the
actor's own voice,

112
00:03:41,602 --> 00:03:42,589
almost routine.

113
00:03:42,902 --> 00:03:45,411
It also powers a new
kind of scam: a

114
00:03:45,481 --> 00:03:47,153
phone call from your
daughter asking for

115
00:03:47,211 --> 00:03:47,652
money.

116
00:03:48,163 --> 00:03:50,984
A simple defense:
agree on a family code

117
00:03:51,008 --> 00:03:53,248
word that only real
relatives know.

118
00:03:53,809 --> 00:03:54,842
Music followed.

119
00:03:55,202 --> 00:03:57,270
Type a sentence, get
a full song with

120
00:03:57,305 --> 00:03:57,908
vocals.

121
00:03:58,268 --> 00:04:00,381
For a while, the
record labels answered

122
00:04:00,405 --> 00:04:01,275
with lawsuits.

123
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:03,645
Then the mood
changed.

124
00:04:03,793 --> 00:04:07,137
In late 2025, Warner
settled with Suno

125
00:04:07,276 --> 00:04:09,842
and Udio, and
Universal settled with

126
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:10,516
Udio.

127
00:04:10,876 --> 00:04:13,500
The fight moved from
'stop' to 'license

128
00:04:13,546 --> 00:04:15,218
it, and share
the money.'

129
00:04:15,697 --> 00:04:17,740
Whether artists
really benefit from those

130
00:04:17,786 --> 00:04:19,389
deals is still
being argued.

131
00:04:19,702 --> 00:04:23,301
But the era of
ignoring AI music is over.

132
00:04:23,964 --> 00:04:25,891
The most important
change, though, was

133
00:04:25,938 --> 00:04:27,691
not a picture
or a song.

134
00:04:28,051 --> 00:04:29,339
It was a chat box.

135
00:04:29,883 --> 00:04:31,880
You write in plain
language, and it

136
00:04:31,927 --> 00:04:32,565
answers:

137
00:04:33,053 --> 00:04:34,678
it explains tax
letters,

138
00:04:35,166 --> 00:04:37,802
fixes a spreadsheet
formula, drafts an

139
00:04:37,872 --> 00:04:40,913
email, helps you
learn a language, writes

140
00:04:40,995 --> 00:04:41,564
code.

141
00:04:42,132 --> 00:04:44,071
Assistants now sit
inside the tools

142
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:46,555
people already use:
documents,

143
00:04:46,892 --> 00:04:47,821
spreadsheets,

144
00:04:48,285 --> 00:04:50,968
search, phones and
programming editors.

145
00:04:51,507 --> 00:04:53,074
And they are changing
shape.

146
00:04:53,527 --> 00:04:55,373
At first they
only talked.

147
00:04:55,919 --> 00:04:56,267
Now

148
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:57,475
they act.

149
00:04:58,102 --> 00:04:59,808
An agent is an
assistant that can use

150
00:04:59,843 --> 00:05:03,582
tools: browse the
web, open files, run

151
00:05:03,675 --> 00:05:07,031
code, fill in forms,
and keep going until

152
00:05:07,089 --> 00:05:08,169
the task is done.

153
00:05:08,714 --> 00:05:11,315
The first experiments
in 2023, like

154
00:05:11,361 --> 00:05:14,194
AutoGPT, looped
and got lost.

155
00:05:14,763 --> 00:05:16,842
Now agents can work
on programming tasks

156
00:05:16,876 --> 00:05:18,247
for hours at a time.

157
00:05:18,378 --> 00:05:21,617
One research group,
METR, has measured

158
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:23,893
that the length of
tasks AI can finish on

159
00:05:23,939 --> 00:05:26,087
its own has been
doubling every few

160
00:05:26,145 --> 00:05:26,726
months.

161
00:05:27,286 --> 00:05:28,969
That does not make
them reliable.

162
00:05:29,202 --> 00:05:31,617
They still make
confident mistakes, which

163
00:05:31,664 --> 00:05:33,940
is why the best
results come from a human

164
00:05:33,998 --> 00:05:35,229
who checks the work.

165
00:05:35,746 --> 00:05:37,592
Which brings us to
the question everyone

166
00:05:37,639 --> 00:05:40,030
asks: what happens
to jobs?

167
00:05:40,611 --> 00:05:42,910
So far the picture
is mixed, but one

168
00:05:42,956 --> 00:05:44,372
pattern keeps
showing up.

169
00:05:44,954 --> 00:05:47,531
Stanford economists
studying payroll data

170
00:05:47,764 --> 00:05:49,447
found that young
workers in the most

171
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:52,442
AI-exposed jobs lost
ground, mostly

172
00:05:52,489 --> 00:05:54,730
because companies
hired fewer beginners,

173
00:05:54,962 --> 00:05:56,634
not because they
fired people.

174
00:05:57,154 --> 00:05:59,755
That matters, because
entry-level work is

175
00:05:59,802 --> 00:06:01,346
where careers start.

176
00:06:01,543 --> 00:06:04,272
If the simple tasks
go to software, how

177
00:06:04,318 --> 00:06:06,443
does anyone learn
the hard ones?

178
00:06:06,947 --> 00:06:08,502
History has examples.

179
00:06:08,862 --> 00:06:11,254
Telephone
switchboards, typesetting and

180
00:06:11,323 --> 00:06:13,287
film developing once
employed huge

181
00:06:13,333 --> 00:06:14,273
numbers of people.

182
00:06:14,691 --> 00:06:17,153
New jobs appeared,
but not for the same

183
00:06:17,199 --> 00:06:19,974
people, and not at
the same time.

184
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:23,030
The honest answer is
that nobody knows

185
00:06:23,077 --> 00:06:24,191
the final number.

186
00:06:24,315 --> 00:06:26,580
What we do know is
that the valuable

187
00:06:26,603 --> 00:06:27,845
skills are shifting:

188
00:06:28,333 --> 00:06:32,130
judgment, taste,
knowing what to ask, and

189
00:06:32,188 --> 00:06:33,708
checking what
comes back.

190
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:35,508
Now the big question.

191
00:06:35,868 --> 00:06:38,109
Today's systems are
narrow in a strange

192
00:06:38,155 --> 00:06:38,678
way:

193
00:06:39,166 --> 00:06:41,802
brilliant at some
tasks, clueless at

194
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:42,382
others.

195
00:06:42,963 --> 00:06:45,831
Artificial general
intelligence, or AGI,

196
00:06:45,912 --> 00:06:47,735
means a system that
can learn and reason

197
00:06:47,793 --> 00:06:50,277
across almost any
intellectual task as

198
00:06:50,347 --> 00:06:51,775
well as a capable
human.

199
00:06:52,295 --> 00:06:53,399
When could that
happen?

200
00:06:53,817 --> 00:06:55,581
Experts disagree
wildly.

201
00:06:55,848 --> 00:06:57,881
Some lab leaders say
within a few years.

202
00:06:58,299 --> 00:07:00,713
A large 2023 survey
of researchers put

203
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:03,593
the median guess
around 2047, and many

204
00:07:03,651 --> 00:07:05,369
forecasts have moved
earlier since.

205
00:07:05,857 --> 00:07:07,564
Others doubt today's
approach gets there

206
00:07:07,587 --> 00:07:08,075
at all.

207
00:07:08,629 --> 00:07:09,418
Nobody knows.

208
00:07:09,906 --> 00:07:11,357
But the people
building these systems

209
00:07:11,403 --> 00:07:12,460
agree on one thing:

210
00:07:12,878 --> 00:07:14,921
if it happens, it
will not be like

211
00:07:14,956 --> 00:07:16,163
another app update.

212
00:07:16,744 --> 00:07:19,229
It could speed up
science, medicine and

213
00:07:19,310 --> 00:07:20,135
clean energy.

214
00:07:20,623 --> 00:07:23,212
It could also
concentrate power, and in

215
00:07:23,247 --> 00:07:25,743
the wrong hands,
cause real harm.

216
00:07:26,290 --> 00:07:27,858
That is why
governments are writing

217
00:07:27,916 --> 00:07:28,624
rules.

218
00:07:28,764 --> 00:07:31,005
Europe's AI Act has
been phasing in since

219
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:34,384
2024, and labs now
run safety tests

220
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,729
before releasing
their strongest models.

221
00:07:37,314 --> 00:07:39,439
Meanwhile, there is
something you can do

222
00:07:39,509 --> 00:07:42,353
today: use these
tools, learn what they

223
00:07:42,388 --> 00:07:44,721
are good at, and
notice where they fail.

224
00:07:45,233 --> 00:07:47,707
And to stay current,
follow a few good

225
00:07:47,765 --> 00:07:50,807
sources, and be
skeptical of anyone who

226
00:07:50,877 --> 00:07:52,467
sounds completely
certain.

227
00:07:53,048 --> 00:07:54,975
The curve is steep
right now.

228
00:07:55,556 --> 00:07:57,901
The real question is
not whether AI

229
00:07:57,982 --> 00:07:59,050
changes everything.

230
00:07:59,468 --> 00:08:01,849
It is who gets to
decide where the curve

231
00:08:01,883 --> 00:08:02,685
levels off.

232
00:08:03,233 --> 00:08:05,427
Tell us in the
comments: what is the

233
00:08:05,497 --> 00:08:08,446
first thing you would
hand over to an AI?

